(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-drill-anti-infectives"]={"title":"Ocular Anti-infectives Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is soothing on an inflamed eye, so it may be used even when bacterial infection is not confirmed?","choices":["Erythromycin","Azithromycin","Ciprofloxacin","Tobramycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is soothing on the inflamed eye, and it is dirt cheap. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is used for prophylaxis of ophthalmia neonatorum?","choices":["Gatifloxacin","Erythromycin","Sulfacetamide","Bacitracin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The 0.5% ointment, for superficial conjunctival or corneal infection and for newborn prophylaxis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is considerably more expensive and given less frequently, so it is not used as often clinically?","choices":["Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Twice a day against four or more for erythromycin, but the cost keeps it off the shelf. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic leaves a white precipitate in about one in six patients?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Ofloxacin","Levofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A white precipitate in roughly 17%, which is specific to it among the fluoroquinolones. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class must be avoided in a patient with a sulfonamide allergy?","choices":["Sulfacetamide","Bacitracin","Tobramycin","Trimethoprim with polymyxin B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a sulfonamide, so a sulfa allergy rules it out. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by preventing transfer of mucopeptides into the growing wall?","choices":["Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Gentamicin","Ofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A cell wall agent, which sets it apart from the protein-synthesis and DNA agents alongside it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class binds the 50S ribosome and blocks transpeptidation?","choices":["Tobramycin","Ciprofloxacin","Erythromycin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The macrolide mechanism: no protein synthesis, so the bacterium dies. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class binds the 30S subunit to interfere with protein synthesis?","choices":["Azithromycin","Bacitracin","Moxifloxacin","Gentamicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycoside mechanism. Its ocular risk is corneal ulceration and reactive keratoconjunctivitis after several days. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, breaking double-stranded DNA?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Erythromycin","Gentamicin","Natamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The fluoroquinolone mechanism: supercoiled DNA cannot relax, and the strand breaks. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic agent antagonises PABA to block folic acid synthesis?","choices":["Trimethoprim","Sulfacetamide","Polymyxin B","Bacitracin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Interfering with folate synthesis stops bacterial growth. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which agent binds phospholipids on the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane and lets intracellular contents leak out?","choices":["Trimethoprim","Bacitracin","Polymyxin B","Erythromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A membrane-permeability agent, partnered with trimethoprim in the combination drop. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class is PREFERRED for corneal ulcers or suspected Pseudomonas aeruginosa?","choices":["Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Sulfacetamide","Moxifloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The fluoroquinolones are the reach for ulcers and for the gram-negative rod, though they are expensive and resistance is emerging. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has conjunctivitis and keratitis has been excluded. Which antibiotic class is preferred, and why?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones, because contact lens wearers are at high risk for Pseudomonas. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class carries a risk of corneal ulceration and reactive keratoconjunctivitis after several days of use?","choices":["Erythromycin","Tobramycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycoside-specific risk, and one of the few adverse effects worth attaching to a single class. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class is associated with an unpleasant taste after instillation?","choices":["Bacitracin","Erythromycin","Levofloxacin","Gentamicin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A fluoroquinolone effect, and it reflects nasolacrimal drainage carrying drug to the throat. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which is the ONLY commercially available ophthalmic antifungal?","choices":["Amphotericin B","Fluconazole","Miconazole","Natamycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Everything else has to be compounded by a specialty pharmacy or given systemically. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which antifungal increases fungal cell membrane permeability by binding sterol?","choices":["Natamycin","Fluconazole","Trifluridine","Ganciclovir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It covers Aspergillus, Candida, Cephalosporium, Fusarium and Penicillium. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which topical antiviral interferes with viral replication by inhibiting thymidylate synthetase and taking the place of thymidine in viral DNA?","choices":["Ganciclovir","Trifluridine","Acyclovir","Foscarnet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Used for herpes simplex keratoconjunctivitis. Its ocular toxicity is punctate keratopathy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which antiviral competitively inhibits the binding of deoxyguanosine triphosphate to DNA polymerase?","choices":["Trifluridine","Valacyclovir","Ganciclovir","Cidofovir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Used for herpetic keratitis, and for cytomegalovirus retinitis by intravitreal injection. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which antiviral is given INTRAVENOUSLY for cytomegalovirus retinitis?","choices":["Trifluridine","Famciclovir","Natamycin","Foscarnet"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cytomegalovirus retinitis is the intravenous indication, alongside ganciclovir and cidofovir. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Adenoviral conjunctivitis is diagnosed. Which antiviral is indicated?","choices":["None — there is no antiviral for adenoviral conjunctivitis","Trifluridine","Ganciclovir","Valacyclovir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It runs a self-limited course and is treated with symptomatic relief of irritation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which route of ocular drug delivery circumvents absorption entirely for an immediate local effect?","choices":["Topical","Intravitreal injection or device","Subconjunctival injection","Intraocular (intracameral) injection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Used for endophthalmitis, retinitis and age-related macular degeneration. Its limitation is retinal toxicity. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which ocular route risks nasal mucosal toxicity and systemic side effects through nasolacrimal absorption?","choices":["Intravitreal injection","Retrobulbar injection","Topical","Intracameral injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Convenient, economical and relatively safe, but what drains down the nasolacrimal duct is absorbed systemically and skips first-pass metabolism. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which prodrug is converted in the eye to epinephrine?","choices":["Latanoprost","Brimonidine","Timolol","Dipivefrin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One of the two ocular prodrug examples; the other is latanoprost, converted to prostaglandin F2 alpha. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which drug accumulates in the eye and produces a bull's eye lesion?","choices":["Chloroquine","Latanoprost","Timolol","Dorzolamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The example given of a drug distributing into and accumulating in the eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Absorption across the cornea is governed by which law?","choices":["Poiseuille's law","Fick's law","Starling's law","Laplace's law"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Transcorneal absorption is necessary for local ocular effect, and its rate depends most on the concentration gradient. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
